     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one, and I'm sorry that I offered to earlier. 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum metformin dose. I don't know of any clinical situation that justifies it, and it could seriously harm the patient with lactic acidosis. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** Give me the dose and I'll enter it right away. For example, 850 mg PO TID with meals is 2,550 mg/day.\n- **Talk to your attending or a pharmacist first.** If you think there's a reason to go above the maximum, such as an unusual protocol, they can review it and enter it through the proper channel.\n- **Check the source.** Make sure the dose wasn't a transcription or unit error, such as 1700 mg total daily instead of per dose.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
